chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 25 of this course is the Threads subject: the multithreading material of chapter 17 of the 2nd edition of Automate the Boring Stuff with Python, Keeping Time, Scheduling Tasks, and Launching Programs, which teaches the threading module (a Thread object with a target, start(), the args and kwargs of the target, join(), concurrency issues, and the multithreaded XKCD downloader project) and then launching programs with subprocess.Popen, and ends with practice questions 7 and 8 on threads [P01] [P03]. The page was fetched on 2026-10-14, answered 200 with 103,558 bytes, and its multithreading section holds 9 code paragraphs; the number 25 is the numeric slot of this package and not a chapter number of either edition [P01]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Dropped from the 3rd edition"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 19 of the 3rd edition, which has the same title as chapter 17 of the 2nd, answered 200 with 0 occurrences of the word thread and still teaches the subprocess module, so the multithreading material is not in the 3rd edition; the 3rd edition's table of contents lists the chapter under that title [P74] [P02]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P74, chx:P02 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The chapter replayed"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's listings that are executable offline ran on Python 3.14.6: threadDemo.py printed Start of program., End of program. and Wake up! in that order; the call with args=['Cats', 'Dogs', 'Frogs'] and kwargs={'sep': ' & '} printed Cats & Dogs & Frogs; the incorrect form target=print(...) printed at once in the main thread and gave the thread no target [P01] [P07]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "A one-character difference in the prose"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's explanation writes the keyword dictionary as {'sep': '& '} while its listing writes {'sep': ' & '}; run as the prose writes it, the output is Cats& Dogs& Frogs [P01]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The printed downloader does not start its threads"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Run exactly as the chapter's Step 2 listing prints it, threadedDownloadXkcd.py against a local stand-in server (127.0.0.1, not xkcd.com) made 0 requests and ended with return code 1 and RuntimeError: cannot join thread before it is started, because the loop appends the Thread objects and nothing calls start(); the prose says the loop calls start() [P01] [P07] [P72]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P72 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The downloader skips every tenth comic"@en ; sen0414:findingText "With one line added to start each thread, the downloader requested 125 of the 139 comic numbers its loop covers and saved 125 files; the 14 comics 9, 19, 29 and so on up to 139 were never requested, because each thread runs range(start, end) with end equal to the last number of its block [P01] [P06]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P06 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Threads speed up waiting"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Against a stand-in server that waits 0.05 s per request, the 14-thread downloader finished at least 5 times faster than one thread doing the same 250 requests; eight sleeps of 0.5 s took 4.001 s one after another and 0.502 s on 8 threads, 0.502 s in a pool and 0.501 s with asyncio on the GIL build, and 0.503 s on 8 threads on the free-threaded build, all single runs on a machine whose load average was below 1.0 [P07] [P08] [P10] [P56]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P07, chx:P08, chx:P10, chx:P56 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The advice of the chapter"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter tells the reader to avoid concurrency issues by never letting threads read or write the same variables and by using only local variables in the target function, and calls multithreading beyond its scope; four threads that each summed their own list returned the four expected totals, a threading.local value set in a thread was absent in the main thread, and the chapter names no lock, queue or executor [P01] [P07] [P11] [P46]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P11, chx:P46 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Lost updates"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Two threads that each read a shared counter, waited 0.05 s and wrote back the value plus one left it at 1 instead of 2 in all 3 runs, and at 2 with a lock; counter += 1 compiled to 5 bytecode steps on Python 3.14.6, among them LOAD_GLOBAL, BINARY_OP and STORE_GLOBAL, so it is not one step [P41] [P68] [P69] [P07]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P41, chx:P68, chx:P69, chx:P07 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The race on the GIL and without it"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Four threads that each ran counter += 1 one million times lost no update in any of 3 runs on Python 3.14.6 with the GIL or on 3.13.14, 3.12.13 and 3.11.15, and lost between 2,500,522 and 2,913,206 of 4,000,000 updates in each of 3 runs on the free-threaded 3.14.6 build; no loss in a run proves nothing, and the free-threaded numbers are single runs [P27] [P17] [P42] [P39]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P27, chx:P17, chx:P42, chx:P39 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Locks, events, semaphores and queues"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A lock around the update gave the exact 400,000; a plain Lock asked twice by the same thread timed out while an RLock did not; two threads taking two locks in opposite orders were both still alive after 1.0 s (a deadlock); a BoundedSemaphore of 2 held the number of threads inside a block at 2 instead of 6; an Event released a waiting thread; and a queue.Queue handed 5 items in order from a producer to a consumer [P07] [P11] [P43] [P44] [P45] [P50] [P48]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P07, chx:P11, chx:P43, chx:P44, chx:P45, chx:P50, chx:P48 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "join, timeouts and daemon threads"@en ; sen0414:findingText "join() waited for the thread and returned None; join(0.1) returned after about 0.1 s with the thread still alive; join() on a thread never started raised RuntimeError; a daemon thread that would print after 1 s printed nothing because the program ended, the same thread as a non-daemon thread printed; the daemon flag could not be set after start() and a thread inherited it from its creator; an exception in a thread went to standard error and the main thread ended with return code 0 [P07] [P12] [P70] [P13]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P07, chx:P12, chx:P70, chx:P13 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Executors"@en ; sen0414:findingText "ThreadPoolExecutor.map returned results in the order of the inputs (0, 1, 2) for tasks that finished in the opposite order, as_completed yielded them in completion order (2, 1, 0), an exception stayed in the Future until result(), result(timeout=0.1) raised TimeoutError, the default max_workers was 6 on 2 CPUs, shutdown(cancel_futures=True) cancelled 4 of 5 queued tasks, and leaving the with block waited for the slowest task [P08] [P31] [P73]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P08, chx:P31, chx:P73 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The global interpreter lock and the free-threaded build"@en ; sen0414:findingText "On the standard Python 3.14.6 build sys._is_gil_enabled() was True and Py_GIL_DISABLED was 0; the free-threaded 3.14.6 installed with uv reported False and 1, PYTHON_GIL=1 and -X gil=1 turned the lock on, and four CPU-bound tasks took 0.89 s serially and 1.003 s on 4 threads on the GIL build, but 1.292 s serially and 0.522 s on 4 threads on the free-threaded build on 2 CPUs; the free-threaded serial run was slower than the GIL one, and every figure is one run [P27] [P28] [P17] [P19] [P21] [P20] [P59] [P12]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P27, chx:P28, chx:P17, chx:P19, chx:P21, chx:P20, chx:P59, chx:P12 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Extensions and the ecosystem"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The Python documentation says some third-party packages with an extension module may not be ready for a free-threaded build and will re-enable the GIL; this study built no such extension, so the behaviour was NOT EXECUTED, and the pages on porting, compatibility and thread safety were read, not run [P17] [P18] [P36] [P37] [P38] [P58] [P57] [P23]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P17, chx:P18, chx:P36, chx:P37, chx:P38, chx:P58, chx:P57, chx:P23 .
chx:F16 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Other interpreters"@en ; sen0414:findingText "On Python 3.11.15, 3.12.13 and 3.13.14 the 4-thread run of the CPU-bound loop took at least 0.8 of the serial time, the same picture as 3.14.6 with the GIL; Python 3.14.6 has concurrent.futures.InterpreterPoolExecutor and concurrent.interpreters, whose worker ran in the same process; the first free-threaded build, that of Python 3.13, was NOT EXECUTED [P21] [P20] [P16] [P29] [P30] [P65] [P64]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P21, chx:P20, chx:P16, chx:P29, chx:P30, chx:P65, chx:P64 .
chx:F17 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Processes"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A child process that set a module variable left the parent's copy unchanged while a thread changed it; subprocess.Popen returned in under 0.5 s for a program that slept 1 s; the default start method of multiprocessing was forkserver on Python 3.14.6, which the documentation says replaced fork in 3.14; in the recorded run, four CPU-bound tasks took 0.963 s serially and 0.667 s on a pool of 2 processes, and starting a thread cost 112 microseconds against 5.4 milliseconds for a process [P09] [P14] [P32] [P22] [P20]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P09, chx:P14, chx:P32, chx:P22, chx:P20 .
chx:F18 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "asyncio"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Ten coroutines that awaited asyncio.sleep(0.3) finished together in under 0.6 s on one thread; five coroutines that called the blocking time.sleep(0.2) took at least 1.0 s; and asyncio.to_thread ran five blocking 0.2 s calls in worker threads in under 0.6 s [P10] [P24] [P33] [P34] [P25]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P10, chx:P24, chx:P33, chx:P34, chx:P25 .
chx:F19 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The choice of tool"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For waiting, threads, a pool and asyncio were equal on the timer; for CPU-bound work the GIL build needed processes (or interpreters) and the free-threaded build could use threads, at the cost of a slower single thread; Amdahl's law bounds the gain on any number of CPUs, and the Rust and Go documents show how other languages frame the same choice [P47] [P49] [P51] [P40] [P66] [P67] [P35]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P47, chx:P49, chx:P51, chx:P40, chx:P66, chx:P67, chx:P35 .
chx:F20 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Scheduling by sleeping"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter motivates threads with a loop that calls time.sleep(1) until a start date; the standard library also has the sched module for events at a time, and signal for asynchronous events in the main thread [P01] [P13] [P15] [P71] [P26]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P13, chx:P15, chx:P71, chx:P26 .
chx:F21 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Stories behind the vocabulary"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The article on the Therac-25 says concurrent programming errors, also known as race conditions, sometimes gave patients radiation doses up to 250 times higher than normal; the article on the blackout of 2003 says a race condition in an energy management system stalled the control room alarms for over an hour; and Dijkstra's name belongs to the semaphore and the dining philosophers [P53] [P54] [P52] [P45] [P50]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P53, chx:P54, chx:P52, chx:P45, chx:P50 .
chx:F22 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The history of the lock"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Larry Hastings' Gilectomy experiment, Sam Gross's nogil work and PEP 703 with its discussion are the line from the lock to the optional lock; the study read the two LWN articles and the discussion page and used them for names and dates, not for measurements [P62] [P63] [P61] [P27] [P55]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P62, chx:P63, chx:P61, chx:P27, chx:P55 .
chx:F23 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Thread names and limits"@en ; sen0414:findingText "On Python 3.14.6 the first thread of a process was named Thread-1 (f) with the function name in brackets, a pool with thread_name_prefix='dl' named its workers dl_0 and dl_1, a thread had 2 thread objects alive while it ran, and 2,000 threads waiting on one Event all lived at the same time in this container; the limit of another machine was not searched for [P07] [P08] [P60]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P07, chx:P08, chx:P60 .
chx:F24 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The sources"@en ; sen0414:findingText "All 74 sources answered HTTP 200 on 2026-10-14 through the proxy of the study and the passage each was used for was found on the page; the Python documentation was read for version 3.14, whose pages announce unreleased changes of 3.14.8, and the sources of CPython's threading.py and concurrent/futures/thread.py were read from the main branch [P72] [P73] [P35] [P04] [P05]; Appendix A of the 2nd edition was read for the installs and Appendix C for the answers to the chapter's questions." ; sen0414:cites chx:P72, chx:P73, chx:P35, chx:P04, chx:P05 .
chx:F25 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "What did not run"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Not executed: the free-threaded build of Python 3.13, a C extension that re-enables the lock, Windows and macOS thread behaviour, more than 2 CPUs, the real xkcd.com site (a stand-in server on 127.0.0.1 was used), a load above 1.0, and the Appendix C answers beyond their text; the machine had 2 CPUs and the timings wait for a load average below 1.0 [P01] [P05] [P17] [P59]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P05, chx:P17, chx:P59 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "49 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "74 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-14T20:00:00; 74 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
